d ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-3I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-3I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3e2a0d1a-8aaf-5755-a625-95b675a14933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368746 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186368746 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368746 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56f5bf98-83ff-5a32-b09b-919c5819f0ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368747 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186368747 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368747 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02c9a095-fd9d-56ba-ba47-e3e219dbe6d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368748 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\